WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.771
♪♪♪
- With the second pick, the Denver

00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:08.942
Broncos select...
- Super Bowl champion,
Super Bowl MVP...
- Von Miller.

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:12.512
- Von Miller.
- Von Miller.
- Von Miller.

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:18.618
♪♪♪
- Howdy, guys.

00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:22.088
Welcome to this week's
Voncast. This is your host, Von Miller.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.891
I'm live from Orchard Park in my office,

00:00:24.891 --> 00:00:27.394
and you guys are watching on the Bleacher
Report app.

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:31.631
First and foremost,
I want to say thank you

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:35.802
to Bills Mafia, and the city of Orchard Park
and the city of Buffalo

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:39.572
for digging us out
and, you know, helping out and making

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:42.575
sure me and all of my teammates

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:44.811
made it to our game.

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:45.745
It was pretty crazy.

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:48.782
You know, we got 77 inches of snow.

00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:51.785
And if you do the quick math,
that's about 6-foot-4.

00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:55.655
You know, I was in Denver,
and I've seen snow before. And I've

00:00:56.056 --> 00:00:59.225
been in snow. But six-and-a-half

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:02.762
feet of snow is just ridiculous.

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:05.031
And the only way
that we could have made it out was,

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:09.235
you know, a huge team effort and,
you know, huge effort from the community.

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:11.171
Lucky for me,

00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:14.207
I live right next door to Coach McDermott.
So I just called Coach McDermott

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:17.010
the night before, and I said, "Hey,

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:20.013
whatever you doing tomorrow, I'm with you."

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:23.616
And he's like, "Hey, I'm gonna give you a,
I hit you up in the morning,

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:25.685
and I'll let you know
when I'm ready to go."

00:01:25.685 --> 00:01:27.220
And I just knew I was cool, man.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:28.955
And honestly,
when I moved here to Orchard Park,

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:31.324
I kind of felt like that was kind
of, like, my insurance policy.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:32.125
I didn't think that

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:33.860
I would ever get

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.729
snowed in or anything like that.
But I knew if it were to snow,

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:40.200
if I ever needed a ride to work
or anything like that, then, you know,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.536
I live right next door to Coach McDermott.
And the snow was crazy.

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:45.105
It just,

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:46.639
Lake Erie just dumped on us.

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:49.776
Snow after snow
after snow for a whole 24 hours.

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:51.878
And at first, I was kind of skeptical.

00:01:51.878 --> 00:01:55.281
At first, I was kind of, you know, thinking
like, man, it might not snow like that.

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:57.016
It might, you know,
it might just do a little something.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:02:00.286
But huge snowflakes, nonstop.

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:02.155
24 hours, man.

00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:04.290
It was crazy, man. Lucky for me,

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:05.558
my son was here.

00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:07.327
I live right next door to Coach McDermott.

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:11.364
We had plenty of food.
But that wasn't the case for everybody.

00:02:11.364 --> 00:02:14.000
So I just want to say thank you to Orchard
Park.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:16.269
Thank you to Bills Mafia.

00:02:16.269 --> 00:02:17.770
You guys are incredible.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.440
We had to reschedule our game.
And some people were saying that

00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:23.676
the NFL has gotten soft;

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:26.913
they should've made us play
in the snow.

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:28.982
And I'm like, hey, I don't have a problem
playing in the snow,

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:33.520
but what about the safety concerns of fans
getting to and from the stadium?

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:34.521
You know,

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:37.524
people driving in the snow to and from?

00:02:37.524 --> 00:02:38.791
That's all I was concerned about.

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:40.426
I wasn't really concerned
about playing in the snow.

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:43.163
I mean, football
is just one of those things where you just,

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:44.931
you just tough it out.

00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:46.866
But it was just so crazy, man.

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:50.503
So we had to, you know, pack up
and really send our stuff to Detroit.

00:02:50.537 --> 00:02:54.474
This is, I'm playing in Detroit two weeks
straight. Playing in Detroit on Sunday.

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:56.075
Then we've got a Thursday
night game with Detroit.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:03:00.413
It's been a crazy couple of days,
but without any further ado,

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:03.183
let me get to this week's guest.

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:06.052
This is one of my guys, man.

00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:07.554
This is one of my guys.

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:10.657
I've been knowing my guy for a long
time. Fellow Texas A&amp;M Aggie.

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:11.925
Fellow

00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:14.027
first-team All-American at Texas A&amp;M.

00:03:15.094 --> 00:03:17.530
He holds the record
for most receiving yards

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.466
in a single game and in a season.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.635
He's a Super Bowl champion.

00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:25.305
He's a four-time Pro Bowler.

00:03:25.305 --> 00:03:27.707
He is the Bucs' all-time leader

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:30.210
in touchdowns and yards from scrimmage.

00:03:31.144 --> 00:03:33.913
And I think you guys
can figure it out now.

00:03:33.913 --> 00:03:37.350
It's my boy Mike Evans!

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.452
Mike, what's good, my boy?

00:03:40.153 --> 00:03:41.521
- Just chillin' on the off day.

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:42.956
Thanks for having me, bro.

00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:44.290
- Man, for sure, man.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:48.127
As you probably heard, man, we got 77
inches of snow, and you're in Tampa.

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:50.330
I'm looking in the back.
The weather's nice.

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:53.967
- It was nice today.
- You just coming off a bye week.

00:03:53.967 --> 00:03:56.369
What did you do for your bye week?
- Just went back to Galveston.

00:03:56.369 --> 00:04:00.773
You know, the hometown. Chilled with the fam.
Did a couple community events.

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:04.510
And that's pretty much it, man.
And watched some football.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.113
So it was a good week.
- Did you get a chance to play some golf?

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.115
- No golf.

00:04:09.215 --> 00:04:10.183
- No golf?

00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:12.518
- No golf.
- Man,

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:16.589
I've been playing golf for two years.
And I know we had that Texas A&amp;M...
- Mmhmm.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:20.460
- We had the Texas A&amp;M alumni
golf tournament, and I saw you kind of
- Yep.

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.763
- get the bug, and like, you know, that was
dope to see, man.

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:27.333
Because I was in that same spot
just a year before, man.

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:29.502
You get out there with your boys,
and you start hitting the clubs and

00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:33.273
hitting the balls. And then that
competitive nature just sets in, like,
- Mmhmm.

00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:36.542
- nah, Imma figure this out, brah.
Imma figure it out.

00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:37.644
It can't be that hard, man.

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:40.346
Imma figure it out.
- And you can drink and play golf.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.416
So that's pretty cool too.
- Bro, you can

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:46.019
drink, play golf.
You're out there for hours with your boys.

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:49.155
There's really nothing like it, man.

00:04:49.155 --> 00:04:51.391
And I really just enjoy being outside.

00:04:51.391 --> 00:04:55.795
I enjoy the companionship with my boys
and, you know, my family.

00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:59.732
And it just gives you another thing
to have in common with your teammates.
- Mmhmm.

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:03.536
- It's just another thing that me
and my dad and me and my brothers can do.

00:05:03.536 --> 00:05:06.172
And this is a game that we can play
for the rest of our lives.

00:05:06.172 --> 00:05:09.342
You know, I started when I was 33.

00:05:09.909 --> 00:05:12.912
And you can qualify for the senior tour at 50.

00:05:13.513 --> 00:05:16.082
So that gives me 17 years to become a pro.

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:16.516
- How old are you right now?

00:05:16.516 --> 00:05:19.319
- I'm 33.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.255
33.
- OK.

00:05:22.255 --> 00:05:24.791
- I'm 33 years old, so it gives me 17 years to get ready
for the senior tour.

00:05:25.325 --> 00:05:27.493
And, you know, deep down—
- You still got it on that football field.

00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:32.265
- I'm still trying, man. I'm still trying.
- Thirty-three's a young man.
You look like a young man out there.

00:05:32.265 --> 00:05:35.401
- I think the key is once
you start saying, "I'm old," and once,

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:37.203
like, the everyday, like,

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:41.240
the everyday grind of football
and the things you do,

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.410
once you start telling yourself,
like, "Man, I'm getting tired of this."

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:45.278
Or, "Man, I'm getting old."

00:05:45.278 --> 00:05:48.614
I think that's when you really become old
because your body is gonna do

00:05:48.614 --> 00:05:49.215
what it's gonna do.

00:05:49.215 --> 00:05:51.451
You really have no control over your body.
- Yeah.

00:05:51.451 --> 00:05:54.187
- But your body is going to listen
to your mind too.

00:05:54.187 --> 00:05:57.490
And once you start becoming old,
once your mindset becomes old,

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.058
like, it's just downhill from there.

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:02.962
I mean, you see Tom Brady do it, man.
- Oh, yeah.
- Like, you see him do it all the time.

00:06:02.962 --> 00:06:05.531
He's not old mentally, bro.
Like, the body gonna do what it's gonna do.

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:08.801
But once you become old mentally, it's
not, you can't bounce back from that.

00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:10.069
- And that's facts. I agree with that.

00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:15.575
- Speaking of old, or time, you haven't
been playing wide receiver for a long time.

00:06:15.575 --> 00:06:18.845
You played wide
receiver your senior year—
- Senior year in high school.

00:06:19.145 --> 00:06:20.580
But look, people always think that.

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:22.915
But I have, I played when I was younger.

00:06:22.915 --> 00:06:23.716
I just stopped playing

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:25.985
when I got to high school
cuz I wanted to focus on basketball.

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:29.355
And I've always loved
the game of football.

00:06:29.489 --> 00:06:33.359
It's just, you know, when I got
to high school, I got a little taller.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:35.561
Basketball is my favorite sport.

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:37.096
I just wanted to focus on that.

00:06:37.163 --> 00:06:40.066
My senior year,
I just didn't want to be bored in school,

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:41.768
and my friends knew I could play.

00:06:41.768 --> 00:06:43.736
So they were like,
"Senior year, you got to come out and play."

00:06:45.338 --> 00:06:47.940
I went out and played, and it
was one of the best decisions I ever made.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.942
- Man, for sure, man.
You continue to kill stuff.

00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:51.811
And I know
we're talking about killing stuff, man.

00:06:51.811 --> 00:06:53.379
You're chasing history right now, man.

00:06:53.379 --> 00:06:58.584
You 329 yards away from a thousand yards,
which would be your ninth

00:06:58.584 --> 00:07:03.122
consecutive season doing that. That would
break your own record of eight seasons.

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:04.924
You knew that?
Of course you know that, man.

00:07:04.924 --> 00:07:06.125
Of course you know that, man.

00:07:06.125 --> 00:07:08.494
- Yeah.
- Did you know—

00:07:08.628 --> 00:07:11.831
did you know you're 28 yards away from 10,000 yards?

00:07:12.265 --> 00:07:14.033
10,000 career yards?

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:15.835
- Yeah, I knew that too.

00:07:15.835 --> 00:07:17.136
There's something records—
- Did you know—

00:07:17.136 --> 00:07:19.338
- I'm not aware of, but those records I'm aware of cuz

00:07:19.739 --> 00:07:21.741
you know, that's a
that's a pretty cool feat.

00:07:21.741 --> 00:07:22.842
And hopefully I can get it.

00:07:22.842 --> 00:07:24.610
You know, one catch. That'll be cool.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:27.447
I wanted to do it at home.
- Yeah, you're at home this weekend coming up.

00:07:27.447 --> 00:07:27.613
Bro, did

00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:30.750
you know you're 88 yards away
from passing Shannon Sharpe

00:07:31.451 --> 00:07:35.655
for 50th all time in career receiving yards?

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:38.224
- Uncle Shay Shay?

00:07:38.224 --> 00:07:41.360
- Yeah, Uncle Shay Shay, man. You
88 yards away.

00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:43.629
- Shoutout to Uncle Shannon.

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:45.932
I got to catch you.
- How do you be so consistent, man?

00:07:45.932 --> 00:07:49.335
Like, what,
talk to me about, like, being consistent.
- You know how it is. You're consistent too.

00:07:49.602 --> 00:07:51.170
- I know, I know. I want to

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.973
get it from, like, Mike Evans.
- From me?
- You know, I don't play offense.

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:56.442
Yeah. I want to see how, like,
- Yeah.
- what is your mindset?

00:07:56.442 --> 00:07:59.579
How do you, what is the,
not necessarily secret,

00:07:59.579 --> 00:08:03.449
but how do you do it, bro?

00:08:03.449 --> 00:08:05.251
- I mean, it's a lot of things combined.

00:08:05.251 --> 00:08:09.322
You know, I've been blessed
with this ability, and this size

00:08:09.722 --> 00:08:11.190
and this competitiveness.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:14.227
Because I love the game of football,
and you gotta have guys believe in you.

00:08:14.994 --> 00:08:17.630
So, quarterbacks have to believe in you,
the offensive coordinator.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:20.132
And you've got to take care of your body.

00:08:20.132 --> 00:08:23.202
I feel like each year throughout my career
I've learned how to take care of my

00:08:23.202 --> 00:08:24.337
body better and better.
- Mmhmm.

00:08:25.404 --> 00:08:28.441
- So that's been a plus for me. And,

00:08:28.441 --> 00:08:31.477
you know, just practice. You know how
it is. The game slows down each year.
- Yeah.

00:08:32.011 --> 00:08:36.182
- And that's just what's been happening. And
I've just been blessed to be in this position.

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:40.686
And so Imma take full advantage of it
and have fun as long as possible.

00:08:40.686 --> 00:08:41.854
You know, give it my all.

00:08:41.854 --> 00:08:44.724
- That's dope to hear, bro, because,
you know, people ask me all the time, like

00:08:44.924 --> 00:08:47.760
"Von, like, when you gonna hang it up?

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:48.794
When you gonna do—"

00:08:48.961 --> 00:08:51.264
I'm like, bro, I, it was days

00:08:51.264 --> 00:08:54.033
and it was nights that I

00:08:54.033 --> 00:08:58.004
got on my hands and knees
at the end of my bed, and I prayed like:

00:08:58.471 --> 00:09:01.207
"Lord, please
let me go out here and have a good game.

00:09:02.041 --> 00:09:03.242
Let me get a scholarship.

00:09:03.242 --> 00:09:05.177
Let me ball out in this bowl game.

00:09:05.177 --> 00:09:06.345
I hope I get drafted."

00:09:06.345 --> 00:09:08.681
Like, these are talks
that I have with God, right?

00:09:09.181 --> 00:09:11.717
And, you know, to look back on it

00:09:11.717 --> 00:09:15.755
12 years in, like, I just don't want to, like,
give that gift away.

00:09:15.755 --> 00:09:18.357
I want to, I don't even want to say use it
all up.

00:09:18.391 --> 00:09:22.161
But I want to fulfill,
I really want to take full advantage

00:09:22.161 --> 00:09:24.497
of the talent
and the gift that God gave me.

00:09:25.064 --> 00:09:27.900
And I just want to keep going
until he says I can't go.

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:31.404
Like, I've already played 12 years
and won two Super Bowls.

00:09:31.938 --> 00:09:35.341
Like, it's easy to, like, get complacent,
you know, as you know.

00:09:35.341 --> 00:09:39.779
But for me, I'm just not satisfied,
bro.

00:09:39.779 --> 00:09:43.249
I just want to keep going
as long as I possibly can go.

00:09:43.249 --> 00:09:45.217
And it's not for anybody else.
It's for me.

00:09:45.217 --> 00:09:47.186
You know, when you start having kids
and stuff, you like,

00:09:47.186 --> 00:09:50.256
you want them to be able to look at you
and what you do in your career.

00:09:50.256 --> 00:09:53.793
You want them to, like, lean on
some of the things that you did.

00:09:53.793 --> 00:09:55.261
And I want to be able to tell them
like, man,

00:09:55.261 --> 00:09:58.764
I left it all out there, and I
did everything that I possibly could do

00:09:59.231 --> 00:10:03.202
to provide for you guys
and to take full advantage of the gift

00:10:03.469 --> 00:10:04.170
that God gave me.

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:05.671
Cuz there's a lot of people
that I played with

00:10:05.671 --> 00:10:08.174
that was better than me, faster
than me, stronger than me.

00:10:08.174 --> 00:10:09.508
All of these things.

00:10:09.508 --> 00:10:12.612
But they're not here right now, and I am.

00:10:12.612 --> 00:10:16.082
And I want to take full advantage of
of this opportunity,

00:10:16.082 --> 00:10:20.653
man. And when you get to, like, Year 12,
I'm sure you do the same thing.

00:10:20.653 --> 00:10:24.523
You start thinking about legacy and like:
What do you want your legacy to be?

00:10:24.523 --> 00:10:29.729
And for me, I just want to win
as many championships as I possibly can.

00:10:29.729 --> 00:10:31.998
I really don't care
what people think about me

00:10:31.998 --> 00:10:34.634
as far as, like, the outside world.
But I do care about what

00:10:34.634 --> 00:10:37.637
my teammates think about me.
And the people that really know me,

00:10:37.637 --> 00:10:40.773
I care about what they say about me
when I'm not in the room.

00:10:40.773 --> 00:10:42.341
And those are two things
that I care about.

00:10:42.341 --> 00:10:45.244
I want to win as much as I can,
and I want to get as many sacks
as I possibly can.

00:10:46.412 --> 00:10:46.712
And I

00:10:46.712 --> 00:10:50.082
care about what my teammates, the guys
that I spend most of my time with,

00:10:51.517 --> 00:10:55.021
you know, 80 percent of my time with,
I care about what they say about me, man.

00:10:55.021 --> 00:10:57.423
And what do you
want your

00:10:57.556 --> 00:10:58.224
legacy to be?

00:10:58.224 --> 00:11:00.493
I know you think about it, man.
Like, do you have an idea

00:11:00.493 --> 00:11:03.362
or—what do you want your legacy to be?
- I couldn't have put it any better.

00:11:03.362 --> 00:11:06.699
Like, what you just said is like,
you know, what

00:11:06.699 --> 00:11:10.302
I think all the time and how I feel
about being in this situation.

00:11:11.337 --> 00:11:13.239
But that's why, you know, you're great.

00:11:13.239 --> 00:11:15.074
You know,
the greats have that type of mindset.

00:11:15.074 --> 00:11:17.243
They want to keep going
as long as they can.

00:11:17.243 --> 00:11:19.378
They don't want to, you know, give that gift
away, like you said.

00:11:19.378 --> 00:11:21.514
So, you know, I feel the same way, bro.

00:11:21.714 --> 00:11:25.718
Like, God blessed me with this talent
and to be in this position.

00:11:25.718 --> 00:11:29.155
So I'm just taking full advantage.
- Definitely.
- And keep having fun while I do it.

00:11:29.155 --> 00:11:32.191
Cuz, you know, you can get down.
You know, there's times

00:11:32.191 --> 00:11:35.394
you get embarrassed out here
and things like that. You get injured.

00:11:35.761 --> 00:11:39.365
But it's like, we dream
to be in this position for so long.
- Mmhmm.

00:11:39.398 --> 00:11:39.699
- Like, I'm

00:11:39.699 --> 00:11:43.536
not going to let a couple of plays here
and there, like, deter my confidence

00:11:43.536 --> 00:11:46.772
in, you know, my legacy
and, you know, how I feel about the game.

00:11:46.772 --> 00:11:52.144
So, you know, I just try to be as positive as
I can as much as possible.
- For sure, man.

00:11:52.144 --> 00:11:55.614
And, you know, coaches always say—
not only coaches, but, you know, people

00:11:55.614 --> 00:11:58.317
that really observe the game and,
you know, the

00:11:58.918 --> 00:12:02.855
players that have come before me, man—
they tell you, like, playing in the NFL

00:12:02.855 --> 00:12:07.293
is only a short piece of your life, like.
So while I'm here in this

00:12:07.293 --> 00:12:11.063
short moment of my life.
You know, this, whatever 20,

00:12:11.063 --> 00:12:15.534
22 years or however long I play,
I want to take full advantage of that.

00:12:15.534 --> 00:12:18.370
Like, I want to take full...
- You going for 20?

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:20.072
- I'm going for 20.
You know, I'm aiming high. I'm going for 20.

00:12:20.072 --> 00:12:24.376
Bruce Smith, he played for 19 years,
and he had 200 sacks.
- He's the all-time sack leader.

00:12:24.844 --> 00:12:27.613
- All-time sack leader at 200.
He played 19 years.

00:12:27.913 --> 00:12:32.551
So in my head, like, if I can play 20 years
and I can average 10 sacks—
- And he played in Buffalo.

00:12:32.551 --> 00:12:33.719
That's pretty cool.
- Yeah, he played

00:12:33.719 --> 00:12:34.820
in Buffalo, man.

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:37.456
He's come to my pass-rush summit. He's

00:12:37.456 --> 00:12:39.992
one of the biggest reasons
why I came here.

00:12:39.992 --> 00:12:42.595
You know,
I talked to him, and he was like: "Hey,

00:12:42.595 --> 00:12:45.064
you gonna be in the cold. Like, offensive linemen,

00:12:45.064 --> 00:12:46.799
they're not going to want to get out of
their stance.

00:12:46.799 --> 00:12:48.901
You're going to be able
to get a jump on the ball.

00:12:49.201 --> 00:12:51.937
Josh Allen, they're going to score points."
You know, all of these things, man.

00:12:51.937 --> 00:12:56.175
And that was my, I would say
that, like, that's my OG or my vet.

00:12:56.408 --> 00:13:00.646
And you have a vet too. You have an OG too that's
throwing you the ball every day, man.

00:13:00.646 --> 00:13:03.349
Tom Brady, man.
I'm such a huge fan of Tom Brady

00:13:03.949 --> 00:13:06.819
and what he's done on and off the football
field, man.

00:13:06.852 --> 00:13:11.657
And I actually think that
the NFL is better when Tom Brady wins.

00:13:11.657 --> 00:13:16.595
Like, when he wins, like.
- I think it is when I'm on his team for sure.

00:13:16.595 --> 00:13:17.363
- Yeah, for sure.

00:13:17.363 --> 00:13:18.030
For sure, man.

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:19.565
The atmosphere is just,

00:13:19.565 --> 00:13:22.301
the atmosphere around
the NFL is just great, man.

00:13:22.301 --> 00:13:24.303
And so, I know y'all been struggling.

00:13:24.303 --> 00:13:25.938
Y'all been struggling,
you know, to start the season.

00:13:25.938 --> 00:13:28.340
But, you know, he's turning it around, bro.

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:29.642
He's still Tom Brady, man.
- Mmhmm.

00:13:29.642 --> 00:13:31.944
- And I'm a huge fan of Tom Brady.
I'm a huge fan of you, man.

00:13:32.278 --> 00:13:35.214
How has he changed the way
that you play

00:13:35.214 --> 00:13:38.984
or that you prepare for games?

00:13:38.984 --> 00:13:41.320
- He's so detailed.

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:43.155
He's the most detailed player
I've ever played with.

00:13:43.155 --> 00:13:45.825
So he makes sure that we're all on point
and ready.

00:13:45.825 --> 00:13:49.862
So, since I've been playing with him,
my mental errors have been way lower.

00:13:50.329 --> 00:13:54.700
You know, obviously everybody still makes
mistakes. But, like, our team as a whole, like,

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:56.335
our mental errors go way down.

00:13:56.335 --> 00:13:59.538
And that's going to be, you know, cause
for more success on your team,

00:14:00.406 --> 00:14:01.607
when guys are doing the right things

00:14:01.607 --> 00:14:03.742
in the right spots,
especially with the talents we have.

00:14:04.109 --> 00:14:07.713
So, you know, I'm just more
detailed going into the game

00:14:07.713 --> 00:14:11.250
and knowing the assignments
and the coverages and things like that.

00:14:12.518 --> 00:14:15.087
And taking care
of my body is the main thing I've learned,

00:14:15.421 --> 00:14:17.456
you know, the last three years
I've been playing with him.

00:14:17.923 --> 00:14:20.292
He's always getting treatment
throughout the day. Always.

00:14:20.826 --> 00:14:22.261
- Bro, it's a 24-hour thing.

00:14:22.261 --> 00:14:23.929
Like, it's, you have to always,

00:14:23.929 --> 00:14:26.966
there is no—when you're a professional athlete,

00:14:26.966 --> 00:14:29.969
there is no down time or no chill time.

00:14:29.969 --> 00:14:34.607
So I spend more time
trying to get my body ready to go or doing,

00:14:34.907 --> 00:14:37.743
it can be as simple as NormaTec
or ice tub.
- Mmhmm.

00:14:38.110 --> 00:14:41.146
- I spend most of my day, like, doing that

00:14:41.146 --> 00:14:45.618
stuff. Not necessarily practicing or in a
film room or anything like that.

00:14:45.618 --> 00:14:49.021
I spend most of my time just getting ready
to play in another football game.

00:14:49.188 --> 00:14:51.190
- Yep. You put the time
in, you'll get the results.

00:14:51.891 --> 00:14:52.558
- Yeah, for sure.

00:14:52.558 --> 00:14:54.693
I got so many Tom Brady stories, man.

00:14:54.693 --> 00:14:57.196
And I've been in the league
12 years. I've been around Tom.

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.833
I started to really
love Tom when I got to kick it with him

00:15:00.833 --> 00:15:04.003
outside of, like, on the football field.
I was in the Kentucky...

00:15:04.203 --> 00:15:08.407
I went to the Kentucky Derby,
and they had me as a color commentator,

00:15:09.008 --> 00:15:11.644
a lifestyle correspondent
at the Kentucky Derby, NBC.

00:15:12.244 --> 00:15:15.114
And they were telling me like, "Hey,
can I get an interview with Tom?"

00:15:15.114 --> 00:15:16.682
Like, could I interview Tom.

00:15:16.682 --> 00:15:17.917
And I was like, man, y'all

00:15:17.917 --> 00:15:19.385
just let him chill, man.

00:15:19.385 --> 00:15:20.252
Y'all just let him chill.

00:15:20.252 --> 00:15:22.121
And they was like:
"He never does any interview, man.

00:15:22.121 --> 00:15:23.489
If you can do that, man, it'd be great."

00:15:23.489 --> 00:15:24.223
And I'm like, man, like

00:15:24.223 --> 00:15:27.593
I know Tom. I'm gonna go
over there and gonna ask him, man. And I ask him,

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:29.628
and he was like, "Yeah, sure, let's do it."

00:15:29.628 --> 00:15:32.031
And everybody from NBC just, like, lit up.

00:15:32.031 --> 00:15:32.631
They was like,

00:15:33.666 --> 00:15:35.567
"Bro, is he, bro, he's getting it, he—"

00:15:35.567 --> 00:15:36.568
And it was, like, live.

00:15:36.568 --> 00:15:38.070
And it was, like, right here and right now.

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:40.973
And like, of course, you know,
we had had a couple of drinks, man.

00:15:42.041 --> 00:15:46.378
It was like this dope moment on
national television at the Kentucky Derby.

00:15:46.378 --> 00:15:50.115
We had our hats on, and he wasn't like—
I was asking him questions.

00:15:50.115 --> 00:15:50.950
He was giving them back.

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:54.086
It wasn't, like, me putting a mic up to him
and, like, just one-sided, bro.

00:15:54.086 --> 00:15:55.187
Like, he was into it.

00:15:55.187 --> 00:15:57.723
I was into it, man. And,
you know, he didn't have to do that.

00:15:57.723 --> 00:16:00.426
And he did it for free, man.
And he had to do none of that, man.

00:16:00.426 --> 00:16:04.096
And I kicked it with him
after. It was him and Julian Edelman.

00:16:04.096 --> 00:16:06.565
I think Garoppolo
and all the guys.

00:16:06.565 --> 00:16:08.801
I went out to, like, a couple of parties
with those guys, man.

00:16:08.801 --> 00:16:13.238
I'm like, bro, like, Tom kick it like
how I kick it.
- Mmhmm.

00:16:13.238 --> 00:16:15.374
- I was like, they kick it how I kick it, man.

00:16:15.374 --> 00:16:17.910
And, you know, I had got his number,
and I had to do the pass-rush summit

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:21.146
later. And I had did, like, a virtual pass-rush summit.

00:16:21.747 --> 00:16:25.384
And he was gonna be, like, a
guest on the pass-rush summit.

00:16:25.384 --> 00:16:28.520
Like, our quarterback correspondent.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:32.458
It was supposed to be, like, a surprise
cuz I had, like, 5,000 people

00:16:32.992 --> 00:16:36.795
virtual for the pass-rush summit.
And I was supposed to surprise them with Tom Brady.

00:16:37.196 --> 00:16:38.364
The schedules didn't work out,

00:16:38.364 --> 00:16:41.700
but I still got a chance to talk to Tom
Brady after by the pass-rush summit.

00:16:42.468 --> 00:16:44.970
I asked him for a jersey.
And I didn't even have to ask him

00:16:45.337 --> 00:16:48.841
five or six times. I asked him for a jersey
one time. Boom, that mother

00:16:48.841 --> 00:16:53.579
was there, like, in two, three days, bro. Like,
I didn't have to remind him, none of that.

00:16:53.612 --> 00:16:55.014
None of that stuff, bro.

00:16:55.014 --> 00:16:58.384
And, you know, I ran into him, like, at UCLA

00:16:58.384 --> 00:17:02.321
after he got done throwing, bro.
And I just come to the conclusion,

00:17:02.321 --> 00:17:07.259
like, anybody that doesn't like Tom Brady,
they just simply don't like winning, bro.

00:17:07.259 --> 00:17:11.430
And that's not a good reason
to not like somebody because they win,

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:12.464
and they're not successful.

00:17:12.464 --> 00:17:14.800
Bro, do you have, like, a good Tom
Brady story

00:17:15.167 --> 00:17:17.269
that you could tell us? That you want to
tell us?

00:17:17.269 --> 00:17:18.904
- I got so many great ones.

00:17:20.406 --> 00:17:21.907
He's such a down-to-earth guy.

00:17:21.907 --> 00:17:24.243
Like you said. Like, growing up,

00:17:24.243 --> 00:17:26.645
I didn't like him when I was a kid cuz
I was a Peyton Manning fan.

00:17:26.812 --> 00:17:28.447
Most people know that by now.

00:17:28.447 --> 00:17:30.749
But, you know, when you're a kid,
you're a little naive.

00:17:30.749 --> 00:17:32.151
You don't really understand.

00:17:32.151 --> 00:17:34.787
But, you know, definitely when I went
to high school and college,

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:37.322
you know,
I definitely respected his greatness.

00:17:37.322 --> 00:17:39.958
But when I met him,
he was such a down-to-earth guy.

00:17:40.259 --> 00:17:42.428
He loves his kids. He's a great father.

00:17:42.428 --> 00:17:44.930
And I have a lot of great stories
with him.

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:48.333
You know, I've been with him
for the past three years.

00:17:48.434 --> 00:17:51.637
One cool thing we did,
we went to the, right

00:17:51.804 --> 00:17:54.573
after Kobe, when everything
was starting to open back up,

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:58.310
the UFC had an event in Jacksonville.
And Usman

00:17:58.310 --> 00:18:01.713
and Masvidal fought for the second time,
and Tom hooked it up.

00:18:02.081 --> 00:18:05.751
He got us backstage to meet Dana White,
the president.

00:18:06.251 --> 00:18:09.188
We met a lot of a lot of people
back there, and I'm a huge fight fan.

00:18:09.188 --> 00:18:13.692
So to be that close and personal, and he
just, he hooked it up like it was nothing.

00:18:14.526 --> 00:18:16.261
We had a great time.

00:18:16.261 --> 00:18:20.599
And, you know, that's it. Obviously,
the Super Bowl parade was lit.
- It was lit, man.

00:18:20.866 --> 00:18:23.035
- I mean, everybody was having a great time.

00:18:23.735 --> 00:18:27.005
Hopefully we do that again.
I know y'all trying to have y'all own
parade.

00:18:27.005 --> 00:18:29.942
But we finna get hot,
I feel like.
- I don't know, man.

00:18:29.975 --> 00:18:31.677
I mean,

00:18:31.677 --> 00:18:34.413
I want you to have a lot of success, man,
and win a lot of games. It's just

00:18:34.446 --> 00:18:35.547
one game.

00:18:35.547 --> 00:18:38.250
You know, if we play y'all, man,
you know, I got to

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:40.185
get mine too, man.

00:18:40.185 --> 00:18:44.823
What was, you know, you say, Tom
Brady is a such a down-to-earth guy, man.

00:18:44.823 --> 00:18:51.063
What was his reaction when you gave
his 600th career touchdown ball to a fan?

00:18:51.296 --> 00:18:55.968
Well, during the game, he came up to me.
Well, he was like, good catch and all that.

00:18:55.968 --> 00:18:57.402
And I said, I apologize.

00:18:57.402 --> 00:18:59.905
Do you want me to
to get the ball back for you?

00:18:59.905 --> 00:19:01.907
And then he was like, nah, you good, bay.

00:19:01.907 --> 00:19:04.510
And then, you know, luckily,
our guy Tim Jarocki

00:19:04.510 --> 00:19:06.044
got it back for us.

00:19:06.044 --> 00:19:08.347
But he handled it cool.

00:19:08.981 --> 00:19:09.815
He handled it cool.

00:19:09.815 --> 00:19:13.719
I didn't know it was his, you know,
to my defense, I didn't know.
- You just thought it was—

00:19:13.752 --> 00:19:15.320
- I'm just happy I scored a touchdown.

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:17.823
And it was something I've done before
Tom came to our team.

00:19:17.823 --> 00:19:21.360
So, I just used to give a ball to the fans
in the stands if I scored a touch.

00:19:22.161 --> 00:19:24.563
But he handled it cool.

00:19:24.563 --> 00:19:25.631
We got it back for him too, so.

00:19:25.631 --> 00:19:28.467
- Of course he did, man.

00:19:28.467 --> 00:19:30.669
That's Tom Brady, man. He's a cool quarterback, man.

00:19:30.702 --> 00:19:32.704
Speaking of cool quarterbacks...
- That's Tim Jarocki right there.

00:19:33.805 --> 00:19:35.307
- The other guy right there?

00:19:35.541 --> 00:19:37.476
- That's my guy.
- Oh, him with the hat? Him with the hat on.

00:19:37.476 --> 00:19:38.644
Shoutout Tim, man.

00:19:38.644 --> 00:19:39.611
- He got it back.
- Shoutout Tim, man.

00:19:39.611 --> 00:19:41.780
- And I think that that guy's name is Byron

00:19:42.614 --> 00:19:45.584
that gave the ball back, so, appreciate you.
- Yeah, they had to hook him up too,

00:19:45.584 --> 00:19:46.752
though, for sure.

00:19:46.752 --> 00:19:48.253
You know, Tom is a cool quarterback, man.

00:19:48.253 --> 00:19:48.820
And speaking of cool

00:19:48.820 --> 00:19:52.724
quarterbacks, man. You played with another
one of my favorite quarterbacks, man.

00:19:52.724 --> 00:19:54.226
Johnny Manziel, man.

00:19:54.226 --> 00:19:55.160
- He the man.

00:19:55.160 --> 00:19:56.094
- I love Johnny, man.

00:19:56.094 --> 00:19:59.198
I love Johnny, man. That's my guy, man.

00:19:59.198 --> 00:20:02.568
I didn't get a chance to play with Johnny

00:20:02.734 --> 00:20:03.502
in all of my time—

00:20:03.502 --> 00:20:05.370
- I know. I wish we got to play
with you, man.
That would have been so dope.

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:08.807
- Bro, me, you,
- We won the natty.
- man, Johnny, man.

00:20:08.807 --> 00:20:11.443
It just would have been crazy,
man. Like, man.

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:12.511
And

00:20:12.844 --> 00:20:16.582
I was in the league, and just, College Station
just changed when Johnny got there.
- Yeah.

00:20:16.582 --> 00:20:18.850
- Like, it just totally changed.

00:20:18.850 --> 00:20:21.720
Like, he brought, like, such a different light

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:24.723
upon College Station and Texas A&amp;M.

00:20:24.723 --> 00:20:28.660
And I think still to this day, they
still are, like, living

00:20:28.927 --> 00:20:32.931
from the light and exposure that Johnny
put on that, on our franchise, man.

00:20:32.931 --> 00:20:33.565
Like, he

00:20:33.565 --> 00:20:37.669
definitely touched our franchise,
and he only played there two years, right?
- Two years, yeah.

00:20:37.669 --> 00:20:41.406
- He only played two full seasons
and he was a redshirt before.
- We came in together. Yep.

00:20:42.241 --> 00:20:44.343
- Yeah, he wasn't even there
that long, man.

00:20:44.343 --> 00:20:47.646
And I didn't get to play
or be a teammate with Johnny.

00:20:48.513 --> 00:20:50.549
You know,
the Aggie network is just so strong, man.

00:20:50.549 --> 00:20:51.183
And I got to kick it

00:20:51.183 --> 00:20:54.653
with Johnny off the football field,
and I got to, you know, know him as a person, man.

00:20:54.653 --> 00:20:56.688
And that's like, that's my guy, man.

00:20:56.688 --> 00:21:00.025
Talk about some of the, you know, talk about
your time playing with him at Texas A&amp;M.

00:21:00.025 --> 00:21:01.059
Y'all won a lot of big games.

00:21:01.059 --> 00:21:05.197
Beat Alabama. Like, how was playing
with Johnny, man, at Texas A&amp;M?

00:21:05.497 --> 00:21:06.832
- He was unbelievable.

00:21:06.832 --> 00:21:09.001
Well, right away, you know,
we redshirted in 2011.

00:21:09.001 --> 00:21:13.005
We came in together, and just right away,
I knew he was different.

00:21:13.672 --> 00:21:15.941
Cuz he could, he was a freak athlete.

00:21:16.074 --> 00:21:20.012
You know, he could hoop. Played baseball,
and he could make all the throws

00:21:20.012 --> 00:21:21.647
on the run and in the pocket.

00:21:21.647 --> 00:21:22.547
You know, he was a little smaller

00:21:22.547 --> 00:21:25.517
quarterback, but,
you know, he used to give our defense fits.

00:21:25.584 --> 00:21:27.786
Our first-team defense.
Me and him would be on a scout team.

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:31.757
They'd make Johnny Manziel go live.
And he'd just be all over the field,

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:33.091
just making plays.

00:21:33.091 --> 00:21:35.627
And he just was different.

00:21:35.627 --> 00:21:37.863
And he made it look so easy out there.

00:21:38.964 --> 00:21:41.767
He just was
always confident going into every game,

00:21:42.134 --> 00:21:45.504
and it was just fun to, you know, play with him
for those two years

00:21:45.837 --> 00:21:47.839
and, you know,
change the culture down there in Aggie land.

00:21:49.174 --> 00:21:50.842
- So, the biggest game that I played

00:21:50.842 --> 00:21:55.480
in, I would say I played in three
games that were huge, man.

00:21:55.781 --> 00:21:58.984
My senior year...
- Nebraska?
 - Would be Nebraska. Six,

00:21:58.984 --> 00:22:03.021
I think we beat them 6 to 3
or 9 to 6 or something like that.

00:22:03.021 --> 00:22:04.556
No touchdowns were scored.

00:22:04.556 --> 00:22:06.892
It was a nationally televised game.

00:22:07.159 --> 00:22:09.628
They were ranked. I think they were Top 10
or something like that.

00:22:09.628 --> 00:22:12.064
We beat them. OU at home.

